<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter Triage
<br class="ltx_break">An empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>Security &amp; ML Workshop (draft for submission); October 2026; </span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Juan S. Santillana
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:juan@jsantillana.com">juan@jsantillana.com</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Independent research</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Perimeter triage faces a latency/semantics trade-off: signature engines are fast and format-safe but blind to unseen attacks, while generative models are semantic but slow and produce unparseable output. In late 2026 a class of commercial <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">System One</em> decision models (TypeSafe Jev; Microsoft-Decision-1) began selling typed decision primitives—binary gates, closed-set choices, ordinal scores—as product APIs, with no academic treatment or independent evaluation. We present <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">VX-One</em>: a small multi-head network (100K–10M parameters) whose outputs <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">are</em> these primitives—a calibrated <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gate</em>, a <em id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">choice</em> over a closed candidate set that always includes an explicit <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option, and an ordinal <em id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score</em> (conditional ordinal regression), trained multi-task, temperature-calibrated per head (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.7–1.7 ms single-event latency p50 on CPU). We evaluate it on three public benchmarks (UNSW-NB15, CIC-IDS2017, EMBER2024) against calibrated XGBoost, Random-Forest, MLP, and Kitsune baselines, and measure a two-phase cascade (Suricata with 52,674 Emerging-Threats rules <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> VX-One) over five days of CIC-IDS2017. Our findings are deliberately double-sided: (i) VX-One wins <em id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detection</em> (AUROC/FPR@95%TPR) against every calibrated baseline on UNSW-NB15 and under temporal drift on CIC-IDS2017 (0.711 vs. 0.637), but calibrated XGBoost wins multi-class <em id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attribution</em> on all three benchmarks; (ii) under leave-one-family-out the gate detects never-seen families on 9/9 UNSW folds yet drops below chance on CIC’s <em id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recon</em> family, and an abstention threshold calibrated on one benchmark transfers poorly to another; (iii) on payload-less reconstructions of the CIC-IDS2017 days, signature engines resolve only <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.1% of daily flows—the <em id="abstract1.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gray zone</em> under payload opacity is <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>99.9% of the traffic, which Phase 2 recovers (combined recall <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.978); (iv) probability calibration collapses under temporal drift (ECE 0.04<math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.40) for <em id="abstract1.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> method while ranking partially survives. We contribute not novel estimators but a measured account of where typed calibrated decisions help perimeter triage, and where they fail.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>intrusion detection, calibrated decisions, out-of-distribution detection, malware classification, two-phase triage
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Operating a mitigation policy on the network boundary requires, simultaneously: nanosecond-scale decisions on the line of fire, calibrated confidences that a controller can threshold, and honest behavior on events that training has never seen. Three incumbent technology classes each miss at least one requirement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deterministic engines</span> (Snort <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roesch, 1999</a>)</cite>, Suricata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Open Information Security Foundation, 2026</a>)</cite>, Zeek/Bro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paxson, 1999</a>)</cite>, YARA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">VirusTotal, 2026</a>)</cite>) are fast and format-safe but structurally blind to zero-day and low-and-slow offensives; the recurring failure mode is a rule explosion that no operations team can maintain.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text LLMs for security</span> (e.g., SecureBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghaei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>)— <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bidirectional encoders, not autoregressive models</em>, a conflation we correct—bring domain semantics, but as fine-grained decision engines on the line of fire they are heavy, and decoder-style LLM agents add generation latency and output-format fragility that a mitigation controller cannot tolerate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classical ML for intrusion detection</span> performs well on tabular attribution—XGBoost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin, 2016</a>)</cite> wins our attribution benchmarks—but treats every event as a membership test, producing confident misattributions on families the model never saw (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During October 2026, a commercial class of models positioned exactly at this gap began to ship: <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">System One</em> decision models, sold as APIs that take a structured <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">state</span> plus typed questions and return calibrated option probabilities (TypeSafe’s Jev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">TypeSafe AI, 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">TypeSafe AI, 2026b</a>)</cite>; Microsoft-Decision-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft Learn, 2026</a>)</cite>). No peer-reviewed academic literature covers the class, its evaluation is vendor-reported, and no security-domain assessment exists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper occupies the gap <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">by building the primitives ourselves</em> rather than remoting into a vendor API. VX-One maps a fixed-size event state to three typed decisions in a single forward pass, then evaluates the whole pipeline—including a real signature engine in front of it—on public data with anti-strawman baselines. The contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A typed decision engine</span> (Section <a href="#S3" title="3. VX-One Design ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): shared encoder + three primitive heads—<em id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gate</em> (calibrated sigmoid), <em id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">choice</em> (softmax over a declared closed set that <em id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">always contains</em> <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span>), <em id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score</em> (CORN conditional ordinal regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>)—with per-head temperature calibration on validation data, trained multi-task with a homoscedastic weighting scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unknown-attack honesty, measured</span> (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>): leave-one-attack-family-out across 9 UNSW-NB15 and 7 CIC-IDS2017 families, plus 4 months of EMBER2024 family drift, reporting both unseen-family detection (gate AUROC) and <em id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attribution honesty</em> (how often the <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option absorbs what the model should not claim to know).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A measured gray zone</span> (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>): Suricata 7 with 52,674 live Emerging-Threats rules over flow-reconstructed CIC-IDS2017 traffic resolves only <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.1% of daily flows under payload opacity; the combined cascade recovers it with recall <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.978" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.978</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.978</annotation></semantics></math>, at the cost of a measured precision drop on web-attack days where Phase 1 is entirely blind.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negative results, measured</span> (Sections <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. E1/E3 — Detection, attribution, calibration ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>–<a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. E6 — Ablations ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>): calibration collapses under drift for every method (ECE 0.04<math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.40); class-weight tilts that help weak-label attribution can <em id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hurt</em> it on saturated benchmarks; a fixed abstention threshold is not portable across calibrations; dropping the <span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option raises macro-F1 by up to <math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+11.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>11.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+11.2</annotation></semantics></math> points—the honesty/utility trade-off is real and quantifiable.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our heads are standard estimators; we say so. The claim is about their <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">packaging and evaluation</em>: event-level honesty instead of confident misattribution, measured end-to-end on data with audited labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engelen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanvin et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">System One decision models.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Microsoft’s announcement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2026</a>)</cite> and documentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft Learn, 2026</a>)</cite> define the primitive triad (binary gate / closed-set choice / ordinal score) with calibrated option probabilities and single-pass scoring; TypeSafe’s Jev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">TypeSafe AI, 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">TypeSafe AI, 2026b</a>)</cite> commercialized the framing earlier in 2026. Both are vendor announcements without peer-reviewed evaluation; benchmarks cited (193.6<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster, 444.6<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> cheaper) are industry claims. Our work is the first academic instantiation and security-domain evaluation of the triad; we use their <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">names</em> only for attribution, define our own formal objects (Section <a href="#S3" title="3. VX-One Design ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and deliberately avoid inheriting vendor terminology as if it were neutral vocabulary.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Single-pass inference.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">“Non-autoregressive” originates in neural machine translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>: full output in one forward pass, no decoding loop. We use the term strictly for architecture (shared trunk, parallel typed heads). We do not claim novelty from single-pass-ness—BERT-family classifiers are also single-pass—and our baselines include appropriate encoders; the differentiator is typed calibrated outputs, not one-pass-ness alone. Fast early-exit decision layers for per-event actions predate this design in spirit—the Viola–Jones cascade <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Viola and Jones, 2001</a>)</cite> is their ancestor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration and abstention.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Platt scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Platt, 1999</a>)</cite>, multiclass probability transforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zadrozny and Elkan, 2002</a>)</cite>, empirical per-family calibration gaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana, 2005</a>)</cite>, and temperature scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>)</cite> define our post-hoc protocol; conformal prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vovk et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos and Bates, 2021</a>)</cite> is an optional wrapper we defer as a guarantee, but audit empirically on the temporal split (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), noting that distribution-free coverage claims need exchangeability that temporal splits violate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barber et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Chow’s reject option <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow, 1970</a>)</cite> and selective classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geifman and El-Yaniv, 2017</a>)</cite> ground our abstention semantics: with symmetric rejection cost the Bayes-optimal threshold is <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\lambda_{r}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\lambda_{r}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the threshold we initially set, and we measure how poorly it transfers where the model is overconfident (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Signature engines and their limits.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Snort <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roesch, 1999</a>)</cite>, Zeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paxson, 1999</a>)</cite>, YARA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">VirusTotal, 2026</a>)</cite>, and Suricata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Open Information Security Foundation, 2026</a>)</cite> anchor the deterministic tier. The DARPA-critique tradition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">McHugh, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahoney and Chan, 2003</a>)</cite> set the standard for dataset skepticism; audits of CICIDS2017 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engelen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanvin et al., 2022</a>)</cite> document systematic labeling errors. We use both stratified and temporal splits and audit against the corrected labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ML on network traffic and malware.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kitsune <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirsky et al., 2018</a>)</cite> is the canonical packet-level unsupervised ensemble; we adapt it to flows (documented) and report its Platt-calibrated but weakly discriminative AUROC (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>0.61) as a concrete instance of “calibration creates no signal”. Boosted trees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin, 2016</a>)</cite> are the attribution baseline our engine <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loses to</em> on all three benchmarks—we calibrate them with the same protocol to make the comparison non-strawman. EMBER2018 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson and Roth, 2018</a>)</cite> and EMBER2024 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joyce et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, UNSW-NB15 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moustafa and Slay, 2015</a>)</cite>, and CIC-IDS2017 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharafaldin et al., 2018</a>)</cite> supply the data tiers. Viola–Jones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Viola and Jones, 2001</a>)</cite> is the ancestor of our second-phase-only-when-needed cascade.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>VX-One Design</h2>

