<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{\emph{score}}:\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score</em></mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{\emph{score}}:\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{k}=\prod_{i\leq k}\sigma_{q}(h)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{k}=\prod_{i\leq k}\sigma_{q}(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where the Score head is conditional ordinal regression (CORN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) over four severity levels—benign, low, high, critical—and the choice head is a closed candidate set declared per dataset. We deliberately use <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">neutral</em> head names rather than vendor naming conventions. Trunk depth/width by scale (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,w</annotation></semantics></math>): <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2/224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/224</annotation></semantics></math> (Nano), <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/576" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>576</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/576</annotation></semantics></math> (Small), <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4/1280" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1280</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/1280</annotation></semantics></math> (Base), with reduced input projections on EMBER2024 (whose 2568-dim feature projection dominates the parameter budget); verified parameter counts on UNSW-NB15 are 97K / 914K / 5.9M, inside a hard <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\mathrm{K}/1\mathrm{M}/5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\mathrm{K}/1\mathrm{M}/5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\mathrm{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\mathrm{M}</annotation></semantics></math> budget that is enforced by unit test. The choice head’s open-set semantics are <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em>: where the training data offers a real <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> population (EMBER2024’s 2.19M-file unknown ground truth), the option is a trained class; where it does not (UNSW, CIC), unknown is realized as abstention at decision time via <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\max_{k}p_{k}&lt;\tau_{\mathrm{abs}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\max_{k}p_{k}&lt;\tau_{\mathrm{abs}})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}</annotation></semantics></math> and any composed policy belonging to the mitigation controller, not the network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Training formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-task loss</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}~=~\sum_{q}\mathcal{L}_{q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.441em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>q</mi></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}~=~\sum_{q}\mathcal{L}_{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with a modified homoscedastic balancing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall et al., 2018</a>)</cite>: per-task reference uncertainty
with a modified per-task reference uncertainty so that task-scale differences (ln <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> vs. ln 2 for Choice vs. Gate) do not dominate the initialization. Per-class loss weights on Choice use sqrt-normalized, mean-1 tilts <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with an <em id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">analytic de-tilt</em> in inference (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z-\log\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z-\log\omega</annotation></semantics></math>) applied before calibration, so the recovered posterior remains population-correct. The gate loss is <em id="S3.SS3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unweighted</em> BCE: weighting shifts the optimal logit by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\omega_{+}/\omega_{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\omega_{+}/\omega_{-})</annotation></semantics></math>, an offset temperature calibration cannot undo. Training uses AdamW (weight decay <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> on 2D weight matrices only), linear warmup (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math>) + cosine decay, gradient clipping at 1.0, mixed precision, and early stopping (patience 4) on validation loss. All backbone hyperparameters are frozen across datasets and scales (D11-like discipline).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Decision controller semantics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The controller composes three binary gates from calibrated head outputs: the trained gate (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathrm{cal}}\geq\tau_{\mathrm{det}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>cal</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>det</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathrm{cal}}\geq\tau_{\mathrm{det}}</annotation></semantics></math>, default 0.5); a drop gate on the exact CORN marginal <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>; and a low-urgency gate on the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recon</span> coordinate of the choice head. The severity rubric makes both implications exact—<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\Rightarrow\text{attack}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mtext>attack</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\Rightarrow\text{attack}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{recon}\Rightarrow\mathrm{sev}{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>recon</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mi>sev</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{recon}\Rightarrow\mathrm{sev}{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>—and they are verified with zero rule violations on 3.09M labeled flows, so each gate’s target event coincides with exactly the event its own head estimates.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gates are single-head functionals, by design.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For single-head functionals, post-processing (de-tilt, temperature, thresholding) keeps the calibration error within a bound determined by the head’s own deficiency (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, Lemma 1). Across heads, <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no useful composition bound exists</em>: two perfectly-calibrated heads can combine into a joint policy whose calibration error is <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4</annotation></semantics></math> in the worst case, whatever the marginal ECEs say (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, Prop. 4)—and the <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}B</annotation></semantics></math> case that attains it (two heads estimating the same underlying question) is the realistic one in security. The controller therefore composes <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">boolean decisions</em> between gates and never <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probabilities</em> between heads; any future composed gate must be rubric-reducible to a single head, or measure its own joint calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Thresholds are cost statements, not constants.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With uniform misclassification cost and escalation cost <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Bayes rule with rejection abstains iff <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}p_{k}&lt;1-\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}p_{k}&lt;1-\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow, 1970</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cortes et al., 2016</a>)</cite>: <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> the statement <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (unknown costs as much as a wrong attribution), and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{det}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>det</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{det}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> the statement that missing an attack costs the same as a false alarm. For operators who hold different cost ratios, the mapping is the standard cost-sensitive one, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}=c_{\mathrm{FP}}/(c_{\mathrm{FP}}{+}c_{\mathrm{FN}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>FP</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>FP</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>FN</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}=c_{\mathrm{FP}}/(c_{\mathrm{FP}}{+}c_{\mathrm{FN}})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elkan, 2001</a>)</cite>—e.g., a 10:1 miss:alarm ratio implies <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}\approx 0.091" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.091</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}\approx 0.091</annotation></semantics></math>; we keep 0.5 as the declared default and expose the mapping rather than tune silently. General policies with a full cost matrix live in the controller—max-prob abstraction can deviate from true Bayes when costs are asymmetric (an event with <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> above threshold may have lower expected cost elsewhere)—and we measure the deviation of the abstraction on validation data rather than assume it away.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">UNSW-NB15</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moustafa and Slay, 2015</a>)</cite>: 149,039 / 26,302 / 82,332 flows (official split); 9 attack families. <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CIC-IDS2017</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharafaldin et al., 2018</a>)</cite> (MLCVE-corrected variant): 2.83M flows; two splits, stratified (sanity) and by-day (temporal drift). <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EMBER2024</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joyce et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: 3.85M binaries; temporal split by week, with the public challenge set held out; 2.19M ground-truth-unknown training rows. Label errors in the original CICIDS2017 are documented <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engelen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanvin et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; on top of the audited variant, we repair raw-label encoding artifacts (U+FFFD mojibake in the web-attack labels) and a singular/plural naming drift (<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Backdoor</span>), which a naive ingestion would silently route to the <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> class; micro-classes (11 Heartbleed, 36 Infiltration flows) are retained rather than dropped and stratified exactly. Every count is checked against the frozen schema in code before any experiment runs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">XGBoost (500 boosted trees, hist mode), Random Forest (300 trees, min-leaf 1), an MLP (2<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256), and Kitsune (9-autoencoder ensemble with feature bagging, Platt-calibrated on validation)—all trained with the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> feature stage, the same per-class loss weights where analogues exist, and the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> per-head temperature-scaling calibration as VX-One. This is anti-strawman discipline: boosted trees <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benefit</em> from the calibration fix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana, 2005</a>)</cite>, and we report them raw <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> calibrated. A zero-shot API baseline from the commercial System One class (Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6. E7 — The commercial System One baseline, measured ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>) was planned; it was not executed this cycle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Detection: AUROC and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FPR@95%TPR</span> (the FPR at the threshold capturing 95% of true attacks—an operational line-of-fire number that accuracy-style metrics hide). Attribution: label-aware macro-F1 (GT-unknown rows excluded by label, as they have no known counterpart). Calibration: 15-bin ECE, MCE, Brier, NLL, and reliability diagrams, reported on raw and calibrated heads. Latency: single-thread, batch=1, 3,000 timed events, model-exact hardware recorded with each result.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Infrastructure.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training on serverless GPUs (L4) with atomic checkpointing and tested resumption; evaluation computed <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally</em> from dumped raw logits so that all metric code is inspectable. 60 unit tests including end-to-end smoke training (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{pytest}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚙𝚢𝚝𝚎𝚜𝚝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{pytest}</annotation></semantics></math>, 60/60 green).</p>
</div>
</section>
