<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i) VX-One wins detection, not attribution.</span> On UNSW-NB15 and temporal CIC, VX-One tops AUROC and FPR@95%TPR. On CIC by-day <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> method’s achievable FPR@95 is degraded, but the trees’ degradation is total (FPR@95 = 1.0 — no threshold reaches 95% TPR at nontrivial FPR), while VX-One retains a partially usable operating point. Attribution (macro-F1) inverts this: calibrated XGBoost wins on UNSW, stratified CIC, and EMBER2024—by 4–8 points. <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We report the inversion rather than hide it</em>: our contribution does not rest on attribution wins, and we are explicit about the loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii) Saturation makes stratified CIC a sanity benchmark, not a selling point.</span> All methods land at <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1.0 with ECE <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.001. Headline numbers on saturated benchmark splits are best treated as pipeline-correctness checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Seed variance on the four main-table runs.</span> To size the noise floor, UNSW-Small, CIC strat-Small, CIC by-day, and EMBER-temporal were re-run from scratch with seeds <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{42,43,44\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>43</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>44</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{42,43,44\}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluated with the same offline evaluator. The seed-42 rerun reproduces Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. E1/E3 — Detection, attribution, calibration ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>’s official UNSW row to the fourth decimal (determinism of the pipeline, not luck). Across seeds, AUROC moves <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.002 everywhere except by-day CIC (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.734\pm 0.018" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.734</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.018</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.734\pm 0.018</annotation></semantics></math>), and macro-F1 carries a real spread exactly where the benchmark is least stable: stratified CIC <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.831\pm 0.074" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.831</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.074</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.831\pm 0.074</annotation></semantics></math> (one seed dips to 0.73 while its AUROC and calibration stay saturated) and by-day CIC <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.344\pm 0.085" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.344</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.085</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.344\pm 0.085</annotation></semantics></math> (spread <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.24</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45</annotation></semantics></math>), versus UNSW <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.456\pm 0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.456</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.456\pm 0.001</annotation></semantics></math> and EMBER <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.746\pm 0.012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.746</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.746\pm 0.012</annotation></semantics></math>: <em id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detection</em> conclusions are seed-robust; <em id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attribution</em> conclusions on CIC are not, at any scale—the attribution claims lean on UNSW and EMBER.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(iii) Kitsune: calibration does not equal signal.</span> With Platt calibration on validation, Kitsune is well-calibrated (ECE 0.037) yet near-chance on discrimination (AUROC 0.608). Its ensemble-of-autoencoders signal does not survive hostile-stage re-fit to benign-only data—we re-fit <em id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">its</em> feature stage to its own training distribution (benign only), as a real unsupervised deployment would. Platt on a useless score calibrates a useless probability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Calibration (E3): all methods’ in-distribution ECE stays <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> 0.065 on UNSW-NB15 but drift to 0.39–0.45 on temporal CIC (Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. E1/E3 — Detection, attribution, calibration ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The policy-level implication is precise: <em id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">‘Noul/gate feedbacks are calibrated’</em> (Noul is the vendor’s name for the gate-head calibration loss; we call it the gate, Section <a href="#S3" title="3. VX-One Design ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is a claim about the training distribution; it silently degrades exactly when novel-attack pressure appears, which is the moment the claim was bought for. Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. E6 — Ablations ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> shows the effect is <em id="S5.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">additive on top of</em>, not independent of, class-tilt choices. Classwise calibration of Choice (val) stays <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.007 on all four main runs, and the <span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recon</span> coordinate that feeds <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{recon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{recon}}</annotation></semantics></math> is calibrated at 0.003 (UNSW, <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}</annotation></semantics></math>1,574) and 0.0001 (CIC strat)—max-prob calibration does not imply per-class calibration, so this is a property measured here, not assumed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>E2 — Unknown-attack honesty</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Leave-one-family-out (LOAO): train without family <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, test against <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>’s held-out rows. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports unseen-family <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detection</em> (gate AUROC / FPR@95 without any retraining), <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attribution honesty</em> (how much the choice head attributes to <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span>), and a control (macro-F1 on <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">known</em> families, which must not collapse).</p>
</div>
