<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UNSW: the gate generalizes.</span> Nine of nine never-seen families are detected with gate AUROC <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> 0.85, most above 0.95, while macro-F1 on known families is preserved (0.38–0.56). The abstention rate on the excluded family ramps 3–92%—feature-dependent, not tune-dependent, which is exactly the honesty modulation we want (an unseen-but-gatelike family still realizes <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> 28–92% of the time instead of being absorbed into <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIC: the failures are structural, not accidental.</span> On stratified CIC-Small LOAO (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the gate remains strong where families are textually distinctive (<span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bruteforce</span> 0.967, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">webattack</span> 0.979, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exploitation</span> 0.970, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dos_ddos</span> 0.852), but drops below chance on <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recon</span> (0.426) and near chance on <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">infiltration</span> (0.533, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>): in 77 flow-statistics features, port scans from a datacenter look exactly like a benign port-touching client.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope: what a single-flow triage <em id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cannot</em> see.</span> This blindness is structural, and stating its boundary is part of the contribution: VX-One classifies <em id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">isolated</em> events (one flow’s statistics, one binary’s features). Reconnaissance is by definition a <em id="S5.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">temporal-aggregation</em> phenomenon—low-and-slow scans are invisible to per-event statistics and become detectable only in detection-by-behavior over time windows (event provenance, targets-per-time histograms, scanning divisibility). The perimeter architecture therefore does not rely on the per-event head for recon: Phase 1 (Suricata portscan signatures, which partially cover it) and any windowed detector sit in front of, or beside, the per-event gate (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>); when Phase 1 is payload-blind, the residual recon coverage is declared honestly rather than inflated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The abstention threshold measured at the default is non-portable</span>: on UNSW, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> abstains 3–92% on excluded families; on CIC, where calibration is saturated (Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. E1/E3 — Detection, attribution, calibration ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, ECE <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.001), the same fixed threshold abstains on <em id="S5.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">zero</em> rows—every excluded-family event gets assigned to <span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span> with high confidence. Under-saturated models are correctly selective, over-saturated ones are confidently wrong.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sweep: a portable threshold does not exist (Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</span> We swept <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}\in\{0.05..0.99\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.05..0.99</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}\in\{0.05..0.99\}</annotation></semantics></math> over the LOAO logits already computed (no retraining). On UNSW the sweep admits operating points: <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.5</annotation></semantics></math> abstains on <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of excluded-family rows with <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>55.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> false attribution to a known attack class, and rising to <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.99</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>97.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> abstention at <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.66\%</annotation></semantics></math> false attribution—a monotone honesty/utility frontier an operator can pick from. On CIC there is <em id="S5.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no</em> operating point: even at <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.99</annotation></semantics></math> abstention reaches only <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> of excluded-family rows; the dominant failure mode is <em id="S5.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benign absorption</em> (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of unseen-family events attributed to <span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span> at saturated confidence), not scattering across attack classes (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>, the UNSW failure mode). Median max-probability on excluded rows is <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.999 on five of seven CIC folds (<span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recon</span>: <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.000</annotation></semantics></math>), and the margin alternative (<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{(1)}-p_{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{(1)}-p_{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, median <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.000</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recon</span>) inherits the saturation: margin-based selective classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geifman and El-Yaniv, 2017</a>)</cite> fails for the same reason fixed thresholds do—post-hoc policies operate on the confidence distribution, and saturation has destroyed the information the policy needs. Rescuing a confidently-wrong model requires recalibration on in-scope data (per-window, online), not a sharper threshold; this is why abstention is a <em id="S5.SS2.p6.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mitigation with preconditions</em>, not a guarantee.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30090v1/fig_tau.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/190;" width="429" height="190"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Abstention sweep over <math id="S5.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}\in\{0.05,0.99\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>abs</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}\in\{0.05,0.99\}</annotation></semantics></math>, mean over LOAO folds, on excluded-family test rows. <span id="S5.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UNSW:</span> an operating frontier exists—abstention and false attack attribution cross near <math id="S5.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and large <math id="S5.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> recovers honesty (97% abstain, <math id="S5.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>1% false attribution). <span id="S5.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIC:</span> no operating point—unseen-family events are absorbed into <span id="S5.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span> (<math id="S5.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>94%) at saturated confidence (median max-prob <math id="S5.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.000</annotation></semantics></math>; median margin <math id="S5.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.000</annotation></semantics></math>), so no post-hoc threshold or margin policy can recover them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>LOAO on stratified CIC (Small). Softmax confidence saturates: the fixed threshold abstains on nothing, and unseen families get attributed to <span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span> with high confidence.</figcaption>
