<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EMBER2024 drift: trained-unknown as an honesty mechanism.</span> The EMBER run trains on weeks 0–45 and validates/tests on later weeks; family composition drifts measurably at test time. Gate-robustness is high (AUROC 0.997, FPR@95 0.012). The family-conditional table is the interesting one: <em id="S5.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">berbew</em>, which grew 34.5K<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>92K rows in test, retains recall 0.998; <em id="S5.SS2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wacatac</em>, which mutated, falls to recall 0.129 with <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">85%<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>unknown</span>; <em id="S5.SS2.p7.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grandoreiro</em> (<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=226" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>226</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=226</annotation></semantics></math>, almost absent from train) falls to 0.221 with 78%<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>unknown. The model does not reduce drift into false attribution; the <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option absorbs what the model should not claim to know, and the gate keeps the operator’s safety threshold honest on the detection side simultaneously. This is the strongest single argument for the trained-unknown semantics (and contrasts directly with Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> on two ways of switching unknown on/off in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. E6 — Ablations ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> means under LOAO.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two unknown mechanisms carry the same typed meaning but are not the same estimator. On EMBER2024, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> is a trained class with real ground truth (benign and tail malware), so its probability is an in-distribution rejection statement. Under LOAO on UNSW/CIC, the excluded family is not a candidate: abstention runs on max-softmax confidence over the <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">seen</em> families, which is out-of-distribution similarity detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2017</a>)</cite>—the classic max-softmax baseline, with its known overconfidence failure modes—not a calibrated probability about the excluded family. We therefore report unseen-family <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detection</em> (gate) and <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">abstention</em> separately and never describe the latter as a probability of the unseen event. Both mechanisms lose informative honesty modes measurably (Tables <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>); the design lesson is that distribution shift has no single calibrated fix—the honest object is the pair (gate, abstention), not one number.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
