<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reconstruct five days of CIC-IDS2017 into PCAPs (documented reconstruction, no payload— attacks’ HTTP bodies are not recovered, so content-only signature rules are expected to underperform and this is the <em id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower bound</em> they encounter on this traffic in the deployed position), run Suricata 7.0.10 with fresh Emerging-Threats Open rules (52,674 of them, concatenated) plus 8 of our own structural rules to level the playing field for header-only signals, then measure per family which attacks Phase 1 catches and what lands in the gray zone for Phase 2 to handle. Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> collates per day.</p>
