<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The headline number is the gray zone under payload opacity, not a universal estimate</span>. Suricata with ET rules over flow-reconstructed traffic detects only <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.1% of daily attacks by volume; the <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gray zone</em> is 99.9% of each day <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in this reconstruction</em>, and Phase 2 is what actually classifies it: the combined cascade retains recall <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.978 while precision stays <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.95 on every day except Thursday. We are explicit about what this experiment does and does not measure. It measures the Phase-2 recoverable share <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when Phase 1 is payload-blind</em>—the operational regime operators of encrypted traffic (TLS everywhere, no payload inspection in managed switches) know well—and <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> the real-world gray zone of a deployment with full payload visibility, where Phase 1 resolves strictly more. Second, the cascaded numbers combine Phase 1 and Phase 2 events under a documented independence assumption at the shared-flow level; signature-triggered attacks also perturb flow statistics, so true overlap is nonzero and our combined recall is an estimate anchored at the payload-blind lower bound (Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Limitations ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Thursday failure is the real lesson</span>: on a web-attack day of 456K benign and only 2.2K attacks, Phase 1 is structurally blind without payload (precision 0.059), Phase 2 absorbs all of it, and the FP rate of Phase 2 across 456K benign rows dominates the combined precision (0.552). A cascade is not free: <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> false flag from the blind-detection phase is multiplied by the innocent traffic around it; the correct operating posture there is Phase 2 as advisory (block with abstention, not auto-drop). This measurement—a live-tier engine’s gray-zone share on a public benchmark under payload-only reconstruction—is, we believe, the crudest and most defensible novel number in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Risk–coverage of the cascade’s own gating (measured).</span> We additionally measured the adjudication quality of the composed policy as a per-day risk–coverage curve (adjudicate “attack” iff Phase 1 flagged the flow or the Phase-2 gate exceeded <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>; abstain otherwise; risk = 1<math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>precision of the adjudicated set; Phase-1 leg exact per flow, Phase-2 leg exact per test row, composed leg under the documented independence assumption of Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Findings: (i) the benign-only Monday is a null point — <em id="S5.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> adjudication is an error, so no operating point exists at risk <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>30%; (ii) on the Friday wave (DDoS+PortScan, 41% of flows) the composed policy admits 0.416 of flows at risk <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>5%, the whole plateau supplied by Phase 2 (Phase 1 adjudicates 0.0004 of flows at precision 0.067: under payload opacity it contributes latency and provenance, not adjudication coverage, <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\leq</annotation></semantics></math>1 point); (iii) the small-attack Thursday (0.5%) is where the cascade costs its own gate: Phase 1’s 0.11% flag rate at precision 0.059 corrupts every composed operating point, so nothing is admissible at risk <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>10%, and Phase 2’s clean core covers only 0.44% (min risk 0.3%). This closes the next step of Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Conclusion ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
