<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text branch (a1).</span> On UNSW the protocol/service/state lookups are worth <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5 macro-F1 points and nontrivial AUROC. UNSW’s original feature set is partly categorical field values (proto, service, state), so the text branch supplies exactly the vocabulary a numeric encoder cannot infer. On EMBER2024 the ablation is an identity by construction: the 2,568 input features are already structural PE statistics (histograms, byte-entropy, imports), and no text field exists to ablate—which is also why the EMBER runs drop the lookup tables from the input projection entirely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calibration changes probability, never ranking (a3).</span> Temperature scaling leaves argmax and AUROC untouched and improves ECE (e.g., 0.0496<math id="S5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.0980 when removed, on the same logits). This is the expected signature of a well-behaved calibration layer, and worth saying plainly because calibration <em id="S5.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> our semantic differentiator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class tilts are not free (a4).</span> On saturated CIC, removing the tilt <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S5.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">improves</em> attribution by <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.9</annotation></semantics></math> macro-F1—the de-tilt pushes minority classes <em id="S5.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">down</em> in a regime where the weighted cross-entropy had already pushed them up. On weakly-distributed EMBER the tilt matters less. There is no free class-tilt knob; its effect is deployment-regime-dependent, and our own loss design contains a knob whose default is wrong for saturated datasets. We say so and flag the design revision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dropout is dataset-dependent (a6).</span> on CIC, zero dropout costs <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-14.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-14.7</annotation></semantics></math> macro-F1; on EMBER it <em id="S5.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gains</em> <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.7</annotation></semantics></math> (the majority class dominates and early stopping regulates). No universal recipe; report per dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unknown is a measurable trade-off (a5-wide).</span> Removing the trained-unknown option from EMBER’s 11-way choice improves macro-F1 by <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+11.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>11.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+11.2</annotation></semantics></math> points (10-way is more balanced). “Honesty” is not free accuracy; making the <span id="S5.SS5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option available is a deliberate spend.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span>E7 — The commercial System One baseline, measured</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ran the zero-shot API comparison this cycle against a commercial <em id="S5.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">System One</em> deployment that exposes the same primitive names publicly (single decision model, documented gate/choice/score questions; one API request per flow containing the three primitives over the identical frozen feature state we feed our heads). The sample is the protocol-fixed stratified draw (seed 42) of the UNSW test set: 1,000 benign and 1,021 attack flows, attack quota proportional to family with a floor of 15. The vendor numbers are in Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.6. E7 — The commercial System One baseline, measured ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>; the gate is <em id="S5.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">at chance on this distribution</em> (AUROC 0.568, FPR@TPR95 0.932), choice attribution is equally degenerate (macro-F1 0.069 over our frozen candidate set vs our 0.534), and severity MAE is almost <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> ours. Vendor latency: 208 ms p50 / 591 ms p95; total run cost <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>2.2M input tokens (US$0.09 at the vendor’s published rate). An ablation adding an explicit <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option to the vendor’s choice question—re-run over the <em id="S5.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">full</em> 2,021-row sample so the comparison is paired (identical rows; the only difference is the extra option)—leaves the picture unchanged and slightly degrades it: gate AUROC 0.476 (from 0.568 without the option), macro-F1 0.066 (from 0.069), and the vendor assigns <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> to only 0.35% of rows (0.2% of attacks, 0.5% of benign). The escape hatch is available and unused: the gap is caused neither by the absence of an abstention option nor by prompt sensitivity, but domain adaptation—the generic triad zero-shot does not transfer to network-flow distributions. The paper’s positioning therefore still <em id="S5.SS6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does not lean</em> on beating a commercial model; E7 closes the follow-up by measuring it, and is reported as an external stress test rather than a contribution.</p>
</div>
