<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.7. </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three threads bind the results together.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) The honest triage division of labor.</span> Where the benchmark is saturable (stratified CIC), trees win attribution and a typed cascade adds nothing but a latency reduction; where the deployment regime is adversarial (temporal drift, attack families that did not exist in training), the gate/unknown semantics earn their keep in <em id="S5.SS7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detection robustness and attribution honesty</em> even while losing the calibration war (E3). The contribution is the measured boundary of where typed decisions help, not a uniform “we win everywhere”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Honesty is a distinct axis from accuracy.</span> Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and the EMBER drift table are the clearest pair of the paper: a benchmark-optimal system (no <span id="S5.SS7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span> option, accurate operating threshold) maximizes macro-F1 while <em id="S5.SS7.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">falsely attributing</em> 85% of a mutated family; the honesty-first system gives up 11 macro-F1 points on the choice metric and absorbs the mutation into an honest abstention. These are different products under drift, and the macro-F1 column alone cannot distinguish them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Saturation is a deployment regime, not a benchmark property.</span> The same dataset (CIDS2017) produces a saturated benchmark under stratified splitting and a hard unsaturated one under by-day splitting, in both ranking and calibration. Any paper reporting “F1 0.94” on IDS2017-stratified is measuring the model’s ability to fit a monotone flow of re-used capture artifacts; papers should report the by-day numbers alongside (we do both).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Severity scores are rubric-derived</span>, not human-labeled; the Score head’s ground truth is a documented family<math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>level mapping, so cross-level claims are approximations above benign/low/high/critical.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark data, audited</span> (Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Experimental Setup ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>): LOAO measures generalization <em id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across benchmark families</em>, not robustness against real-world novel attacks; an honest reading of E2 is “the gate generalizes <em id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more</em> than the choice head, on these families”—not “we detect zero-days”.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-seed runs outside the four main tables.</span> The four main-table runs (UNSW Small, CIC strat-Small, CIC by-day, EMBER temporal) were re-run with seeds <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{42,43,44\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>43</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>44</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{42,43,44\}</annotation></semantics></math>: the seed-42 reruns reproduce the official numbers to the fourth decimal (reproducibility confirmed), ranking metrics move <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.02 points, and multi-class macro-F1 on CIC carries a real spread (<math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.83\pm 0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.83</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.83\pm 0.07</annotation></semantics></math> stratified; <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.34\pm 0.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.34</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.09</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.34\pm 0.09</annotation></semantics></math> by-day—drift instability, not implementation nondeterminism). The rest of the tables (per-family LOAO, ablations, E4 latency, E5 cascade) remain single-seed runs; conclusions that rest on differences <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>5 points are robust to this, but the small head-to-head diffs on CIC strat (cf. a2/a4 in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. E6 — Ablations ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>) should be read as noise-borderline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase 1 is a reference implementation</span> (Suricata + ET Open + 8 structural rules), not our C++ engine; the claims of Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> are about the <em id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">architecture</em>, and the reconstruction means payload-content rules are at a lower bound by construction. The “combined cascade” result is a documented two-sample estimate under an independence assumption that is exact for full-family-invisibility cases and optimistic where Phase 1 fails on exactly the stealthiest subset; we state it as an estimate, and the per-day F1/gray-zone table it rests on is a real measurement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calibration under drift</span> (ECE 0.40 on temporal CIC) is measured and now also audited by a walk-forward per-window recalibration experiment (E8, Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>): with one-day-delayed labels, gate recalibration (temperature or 2-parameter Platt) recovers part of the loss (Brier 0.539<math id="S6.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.504 on the hard day), but the oracle bound shows the ceiling is <em id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discrimination, not calibration</em>—even fitting the calibration on the day’s own labels leaves gate ECE at 0.445 with day-AUROC 0.56. Per-window recalibration is therefore a cheap partial fix. Its prescribed alternative is also measured (E9, Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>): one to two passes of per-window continue-training with delayed day-1 labels recover day-2 gate AUROC to 0.80–0.84 (four passes regress to 0.76) and, with the recovered ranking, the recalibration ceiling collapses from ECE 0.445 to 0.10–0.12—but as-run day-2 ECE stays at 0.554 and no delayed-label fit recovers the new day’s composition prior, which lands on the abstention/coverage semantics of Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> rather than on any post-hoc map.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conformal wrappers are scoped, not promised, and now audited.</span> Exact distribution-free coverage applies only under exchangeability: on the official UNSW split and stratified CIC, split-conformal coverage is exact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papadopoulos et al., 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tibshirani et al., 2019</a>)</cite>; on every temporal split, family-composition drift is real and measured, and coverage degrades at a rate bounded by the calibration–test total-variation gap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barber et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. A domain classifier certifies that gap only <em id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">from below</em> (<math id="S6.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 1-2\,\mathrm{BER}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>BER</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 1-2\,\mathrm{BER}</annotation></semantics></math>; Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>)—we therefore report empirical weekly coverage with exact binomial intervals and weighted variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tibshirani et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and make no distribution-free coverage promise; the measured EMBER audit is in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> (binary set-coverage within <math id="S6.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.895,0.935]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.895</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.935</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.895,0.935]</annotation></semantics></math> across all twelve test weeks; certified drift <math id="S6.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 0.15</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.24</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost-per-million</span> is not yet measured from real billing data; latency, not cost, is the number we claim.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-field attack surface</span> (indirect prompt injection through <span id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">user_agent</span>, URIs) is real <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; V0.1 has no sanitization layer and our text-branch vocabulary lookup (bounded 135-slot vocabularies, not free max-k) is only partial mitigation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vendor-API baseline is a single-point stress test</span> (Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6. E7 — The commercial System One baseline, measured ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>); we measured one commercial System One deployment zero-shot on one distribution (dense UNSW flows), not an exhaustive comparison across commercial decision models, prompt variants, or distributions. The honest reading is “the generic triad zero-shot does not transfer to this domain without fine-tuning”, not “the vendor cannot be fine-tuned to this domain”.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We built, trained, calibrated, and stress-tested a typed decision engine for perimeter triage—the first published instantiation and public-data evaluation of the <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">System One</em> primitive triad—and report the boundary of its advantage rather than a uniform win: detection robustness and attribution honesty under unknown families and drift, a measurable gray zone of a live signature engine, and four cleanly-measured negative results (calibration collapse under drift, non-portable abstention thresholds, regime-dependent class tilts, unknown-option accuracy cost). Six former next steps are already measured: the vendor-API comparison (Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6. E7 — The commercial System One baseline, measured ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> — the commercial primitive triad zero-shot sits at gate-chance on network flows, and an explicit abstention option is not enough to close the domain gap), the abstention-threshold sweep (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>—on saturated deployments no abstention threshold recovers honesty, so selective behavior must be bought with data and recalibration, not with post-processing), the 3-seed re-runs of the four main tables (seed-variance <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>0.02 points on ranking metrics; Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Limitations ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and the walk-forward per-window recalibration under drift (E8, Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>—a cheap partial fix whose oracle bound shows the hard-day ceiling is discrimination, not calibration), its prescribed alternative, per-window re-training (E9, Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Formal statements ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>—one to two passes with delayed day-1 labels recover day-2 gate AUROC 0.56<math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.84 and re-enable oracle recalibration, while four passes regress and as-run ECE is unmoved: weight freshness buys discrimination, not calibration), and the risk–coverage curves of the cascade’s own gating (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. E5 — Two-phase cascade: how much of the line of fire is actually a gray zone ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>—on payload-blind traffic the coverage at fixed risk is supplied entirely by Phase 2, the benign-only day is a null point where no operating point exists, and the small-attack day is where Phase-1 false flags cost the gate itself). The remaining next step is a payload-inclusive Phase-1 measurement to replace the lower-bound cascade estimate. All code, configs, tests, and measured results accompany the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Formal statements</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cal}_{A}(f)=\mathbb{E}\big[\,|\,\mathbb{P}(A\mid f(X))-f(X)\,|\,\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cal</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cal}_{A}(f)=\mathbb{E}\big[\,|\,\mathbb{P}(A\mid f(X))-f(X)\,|\,\big]</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> calibration error of an estimator <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for event <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>; the binned ECE (15 bins) is its empirical estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, with its known estimator caveats, which is why we co-report MCE and reliability diagrams and frame multiclass notions through the vector/classwise/confidence hierarchy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaicenavicius et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lemma 1 (closure under single-head post-processing).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If head <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is calibrated for an event <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of its own output and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:[0,1]\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:[0,1]\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is measurable, then
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cal}_{A}(h\!\circ\!f)\leq\mathrm{cal}_{A}(f)+\mathbb{E}\,|h(f(X))-f(X)|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cal</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cal</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cal}_{A}(h\!\circ\!f)\leq\mathrm{cal}_{A}(f)+\mathbb{E}\,|h(f(X))-f(X)|.</annotation></semantics></math>
<em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof.</em> Let <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)=\mathbb{P}(A\mid f(X){=}v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)=\mathbb{P}(A\mid f(X){=}v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Tower property: <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{A}\mid h\circ f]=\mathbb{E}[m(f)\mid h\circ f]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{A}\mid h\circ f]=\mathbb{E}[m(f)\mid h\circ f]</annotation></semantics></math>. Jensen: <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|\mathbb{E}[m(f)\mid h\circ f]-h(f)\big|\leq\mathbb{E}\big[\,|m(f)-h(f)|\,\big|\,h\circ f\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|\mathbb{E}[m(f)\mid h\circ f]-h(f)\big|\leq\mathbb{E}\big[\,|m(f)-h(f)|\,\big|\,h\circ f\big]</annotation></semantics></math>. Triangle: <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|m(f)-h(f)|\leq|m(f)-f|+|f-h(f)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|m(f)-h(f)|\leq|m(f)-f|+|f-h(f)|</annotation></semantics></math>; take expectations.  <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">So de-tilt, temperature scaling, and thresholding do not destabilize a gate’s calibration beyond the size of the transformation; the practical consequence is that each derived gate’s calibration error is <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bounded by its own head’s measured deficiency</em>, and E3 measures each gate’s ECE directly anyway.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lemma 2 (CORN chain rule: exactness and degradation).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With true conditionals <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(x)=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 1\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(x)=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 1\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}(x)=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid\mathrm{sev}\geq 1,\,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}(x)=\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid\mathrm{sev}\geq 1,\,x)</annotation></semantics></math>: (i) <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid x)=\pi_{1}(x)\,\pi_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{sev}\geq 2\mid x)=\pi_{1}(x)\,\pi_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exactly</em>—CORN conditions the second step on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sev}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sev}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so there is no hidden independence assumption; (ii) for estimates <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{1},q_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{1},q_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cal}_{\mathrm{sev}\geq 2}(q_{1}q_{2})\;\leq\;\mathbb{E}\,|\pi_{1}-q_{1}|+\mathbb{E}\,|\pi_{2}-q_{2}|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cal</mi><mrow><mi>sev</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cal}_{\mathrm{sev}\geq 2}(q_{1}q_{2})\;\leq\;\mathbb{E}\,|\pi_{1}-q_{1}|+\mathbb{E}\,|\pi_{2}-q_{2}|.</annotation></semantics></math>
<em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof of (ii).</em> <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\pi_{2}-q_{1}q_{2}=\pi_{2}(\pi_{1}-q_{1})+q_{1}(\pi_{2}-q_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\pi_{2}-q_{1}q_{2}=\pi_{2}(\pi_{1}-q_{1})+q_{1}(\pi_{2}-q_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>; triangle inequality with <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\pi_{2},q_{1}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\pi_{2},q_{1}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The bound is on <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conditional deviations</em>, not marginal ECEs (conditioning on extra information changes the base rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kleinberg et al., 2017</a>)</cite>); hence the drop gate’s ECE is measured directly rather than inferred from its conditional heads.  <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Measured (drop gate, this deployment).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The links’ conditional ECEs do not determine the product’s: UNSW-Small val gives ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)=0.014, ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{2}\,|\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}\,|\,</annotation></semantics></math>sev<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>1)=0.038, direct ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)=0.026, and temporal CIC at test gives ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)=0.42 and ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)=0.14 with direct ECE(<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)=0.11. Inferring the composed gate from its links mis-estimates it in either direction; the controller measures <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> directly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proposition 3 (no-go: marginal ECEs do not compose).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be trivial and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\equiv a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\equiv a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}\equiv b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}\equiv b</annotation></semantics></math> be perfectly calibrated for events <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(A){=}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(A){=}a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(B){=}b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(B){=}b</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cal}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cal</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cal}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for both). The naive AND policy <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=f_{1}f_{2}=ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=f_{1}f_{2}=ab</annotation></semantics></math> estimates <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\wedge B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\wedge B</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup\,\big|\,\mathbb{P}(A\wedge B)-ab\,\big|=1/4," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">sup</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup\,\big|\,\mathbb{P}(A\wedge B)-ab\,\big|=1/4,</annotation></semantics></math>
attained at <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b=\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b=\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}B</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cap B{=}\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cap B{=}\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> (the joint law is free within the Fréchet bounds), and identically for the OR variant. Therefore no non-trivial function of the marginal ECEs bounds composed-policy calibration error; the attainable case <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A{=}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A{=}B</annotation></semantics></math>—two heads estimating the same question, e.g., “is this an attack?”—is realistic in security, not pathological. This is why Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Decision controller semantics ‣ 3. VX-One Design ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> restricts the controller to composing boolean decisions and forbids cross-head probability composition: every gate of v0.1 is a functional of exactly one head, verified rubric-reducible.  <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lemma 4 (BER–TV; drift certification is one-sided).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any two distributions <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}(P,Q)=1-2\,\mathrm{BER}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>BER</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}(P,Q)=1-2\,\mathrm{BER}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BER}^{*}=\inf_{h}\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\mathbb{P}_{P}(h{=}1)+\mathbb{P}_{Q}(h{=}0)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>BER</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BER}^{*}=\inf_{h}\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\mathbb{P}_{P}(h{=}1)+\mathbb{P}_{Q}(h{=}0)\big)</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimal balanced error (the likelihood-ratio test attains error <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-d_{\mathrm{TV}})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-d_{\mathrm{TV}})/2</annotation></semantics></math>). Consequently a domain classifier with balanced error <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BER}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>BER</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BER}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">certifies</em> <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 1-2\,\mathrm{BER}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>BER</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 1-2\,\mathrm{BER}</annotation></semantics></math>: detecting drift quantifies it and plugs into the coverage-gap bound of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barber et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; failure to separate certifies nothing—absence of evidence of drift is not evidence of exchangeability.  <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conformal audit on the temporal split (measured, binary head).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Split-conformal on the EMBER gate’s Noul-calibrated logit (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>; calibration on validation weeks 46–51, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}120" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}120</annotation></semantics></math>k) reproduces the target coverage exactly at calibration (0.9000). Across the twelve test weeks: unweighted coverage <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.895" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.895</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.895</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.935</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.935</annotation></semantics></math> (exact binomial intervals), benign-side <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.905 everywhere, malicious-side down to <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.884" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.884</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.884</annotation></semantics></math> (week 60) — the loss concentrates on the positive class. Per-week feature drift is certified from below: <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq 0.15</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.24</annotation></semantics></math> (held-out BER; domain-classifier AUC 0.61–0.64, so the feature-space drift is real but modest). The weighted variant (clipped odds <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.1,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.1,10]</annotation></semantics></math>, Tibshirani-style quantile) moves coverage by <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0.022" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.022</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0.022</annotation></semantics></math> with no consistent direction — with estimated weights it is mitigation, not guarantee, and here it is unnecessary: what drifts over the four months is the family mixture (label shift, absorbed by <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span>; Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>), not the benign/malicious feature distribution. The challenge set is excluded from these statements and reported separately as measured stress: its coverage on positives is 0.27 (by construction 98.7% <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unknown</span>-family, minimally evasive), with a median gate likelihood of 0.65 — the detector keeps voting attack on most rows (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>0.5 for 58%) but without claiming the set-coverage it does not have.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bayes rule with rejection (abstention thresholds).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With uniform misclassification cost and escalation cost <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected cost of an action is <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(a\mid x)=1-p_{a}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(a\mid x)=1-p_{a}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> under typed outputs, so Bayes abstains iff <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a}C(a\mid x)&gt;\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a}C(a\mid x)&gt;\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., iff <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}p_{k}(x)&lt;1-\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}p_{k}(x)&lt;1-\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>—Chow’s 1970 rule as the 0–1-cost case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow, 1970</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cortes et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, with risk–coverage curves as the primary object <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">El-Yaniv and Wiener, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geifman and El-Yaniv, 2017</a>)</cite>. Asymmetric matríces of cost live in the controller: max-prob abstraction can deviate from full Bayes there (a one-line counterexample: <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(0.55,0.40,0.05)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.55</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(0.55,0.40,0.05)</annotation></semantics></math> with a 3-fold penalty on confusing the second class yields abstention under full Bayes but attribution under max-prob), which is why the deviation is measured on validation data in the controller’s calibration report rather than assumed away. Measured: with uniform costs the abstraction <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> the Bayes rule (zero deviating rows in all three runs with a <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span> class); under an <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-fold penalty on missed attacks, deviations appear in one direction only—full Bayes escalates where max-prob attributes—at 13% of validation rows (UNSW, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>) and up to 29% among attacks (by-day run, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m{=}10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>); symmetric or moderate costs justify the default <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, but ratio must re-derive the policy rather than inherit it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Walk-forward per-window recalibration under drift (E8, measured).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the CIC by-day run (test in capture order: Friday-DDoS <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}138" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>138</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}138</annotation></semantics></math>k, then Friday-PortScan <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}286" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>286</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}286</annotation></semantics></math>k), we re-fit the gate’s calibration on the previous window only (day 1 falls back to the static val; day 2 uses day-1 labels, the honest delayed-labels regime), either as a temperature (1 parameter) or as a 2-parameter Platt map <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}\sigma(a\,\mathrm{logit}{+}b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>logit</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}\sigma(a\,\mathrm{logit}{+}b)</annotation></semantics></math>, and re-derive the FPR@TPR95 threshold from the same window’s positives. On the DDoS day (AUROC 0.99) the oracle bound recovers almost all calibration error (ECE 0.127<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.029, gate fitted on the day’s own labels); on PortScan (AUROC 0.56) the same post-hoc moves are marginal: ECE 0.553 (static) <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 0.526 (Platt with one day of fresh labels), and even the oracle bound — calibrating on the test labels of the day itself — leaves gate ECE at 0.445 (Brier 0.539<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.246: the oracle predicts a constant near the day’s prior). Measured verdict: per-window recalibration is a cheap partial fix whose ceiling <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">under strong concept shift is the head’s discrimination, which no post-hoc map can repair</em>; the choice head’s confidence, in contrast, is fully recalibratable (choice ECE 0.543<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.056 at oracle, macro-F1 unchanged by construction).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-window re-training (E9, measured).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The follow-up E8 leaves open—<em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">re-training</em> instead of recalibration—is measured identically: continuing to train the deployed CIC artifact on its training split <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plus</em> the full day-1 window with delayed labels (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}{+}138" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>138</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}{+}138</annotation></semantics></math>k; frozen feature stage, candidate set, and class weights; one pass at a reduced learning rate, streaming-honest). On the PortScan day the gate recovers ranking: AUROC 0.560<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.800 with one pass, 0.839 at the two-pass sweet spot, and a measured <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regression</em> to 0.760 at four passes (over-fitting to the fresh window). Confidence, however, does not follow: as-run day-2 ECE is unchanged (0.553 static vs. 0.554 retrained), and a Platt map fitted on the delayed day-1 labels cannot express the new day’s composition—it fits a near-null shift (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\approx-0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\approx-0.3</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.7</annotation></semantics></math>) on the now-saturated day-1 scores where the day-2 oracle shift is <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\approx{+}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\approx{+}8</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>. What the recovered ranking buys is an expressive calibration ceiling: the <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> oracle bound that capped static calibration at ECE 0.445 collapses to 0.117 (one pass) and 0.100 (two passes) on the retrained scores, i.e., the static ceiling was limited by ranking, not by the mechanism. Choice macro-F1 on day 2 is unchanged (0.102). Measured verdict: <em id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight freshness buys discrimination, not calibration</em>—scheduled re-training with a fresh-window validate step is a real (if schedule-sensitive) countermeasure to drift, while the composition prior of a genuinely new day remains inferable from no post-hoc statistics and de-facto demands the abstention/coverage semantics of Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. E2 — Unknown-attack honesty ‣ 5. Results ‣ VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter TriageAn empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
